+        # 2026-08-01 panel sweep: a scrolled window must never be a focus
+        # stop on this machine. GtkScrolledWindow sets can-focus TRUE on
+        # itself and installs its own bindings for all four arrows
+        # ("scroll-child"), so as soon as focus lands on the container
+        # rather than on something inside it, every arrow is consumed by
+        # the container and the focus highlight freezes. Measured on the
+        # Network panel, six presses in a row that changed nothing:
+        #
+        #   Key Down:  focus CustomScrolledWindow -> CustomScrolledWindow
+        #   Key Right: focus CustomScrolledWindow -> CustomScrolledWindow
+        #   Key Up:    focus CustomScrolledWindow -> CustomScrolledWindow
+        #   Key Left:  focus CustomScrolledWindow -> CustomScrolledWindow
+        #
+        # Same shape as the console focus trap
+        # (console-focus-trap-and-escape-overlay.patch), and the same
+        # answer: with no touchscreen and no mouse there is nothing a user
+        # can do while the scroll container itself holds focus, and
+        # screen.py's _dpad_scroll() moves the vadjustment directly without
+        # needing focus to be in here at all. One line in the factory
+        # covers all ~25 panels, exactly like the overlay_scrolling default
+        # above. Rows and buttons INSIDE the scroll are unaffected -- they
+        # keep their own can-focus and stay reachable.
+        self.set_can_focus(False)
         self.add_events(
             Gdk.EventMask.BUTTON_PRESS_MASK
             | Gdk.EventMask.TOUCH_MASK
